# Changelog

All notable changes to **rog-cetra-linux** are documented here.
The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),
and this project aims to follow [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/).

## [Unreleased]

## [1.6] — 2026-10-14

**Confirmed on real hardware:** SteamOS (Steam Deck) in *gaming mode* and *desktop mode*,
and Fedora with KDE Plasma. Gestures, tones and the volume overlay all act on the earbuds
only; the system default device is never changed.

## [1.5] — 2026-10-14
### Fixed
- **Confirmation tones now reliably reach the earbuds.** The root cause was that the target
  audio node was resolved to the device *description* ("ROG CETRA OPEN WIRELESS Analog Stereo")
  instead of the real PipeWire `node.name`, so `paplay`/`pw-play` silently fell back to the
  system default device. The node is now resolved via `pactl` (authoritative) with
  `pw-dump`/`wpctl` fallbacks, and a resolved name is only accepted if it is a real
  `node.name` (starts with `alsa_`) — never a description.
### Changed
- VID/PID matching for the dongle (`0b05:1cac`) hardened: numeric **and** hex-string
  properties are handled, plus a fallback name match.

## [1.4] — 2026-10-14
### Fixed
- **Confirmation tones were completely silent.** The tone WAV was fed to `paplay` on stdin
  (without a file argument); `paplay` only reads stdin with `--raw`. The file is now passed
  as an argument, the exit status is checked, and a real fallback to `pw-play` is used.
### Added
- `--selftest`: plays every confirmation tone in turn and prints which audio backend is used
  (`python3 ~/.local/bin/rog-cetra-buttons.py --selftest`).

## [1.3] — 2026-10-14
### Changed
- **No more switching of the default sink.** The v1.2 approach (temporarily switching the
  default to the earbuds so the overlay shows them) was removed: it diverted running audio
  and did not reliably switch back.
- Confirmation tones now use `paplay --device <node.name>` (PulseAudio-compatible), with
  `pw-play` + `PIPEWIRE_NODE` and `pw-play --target` as fallbacks, because WirePlumber does
  not reliably honour `pw-play --target`.

## [1.2] — 2026-10-14
### Added
- Attempted to make the volume overlay show the earbuds by temporarily switching the default
  sink for the duration of a key press (with a lock and a restore timer). **Reverted in 1.3**
  — see above.

## [1.1] — 2026-10-14
### Changed
- Confirmation tones routed to the earbuds by `node.name` instead of a global object id.
  (Turned out to be insufficient — the real fix came in 1.5.)

## [1.0] — 2026-10-14
### Changed — first properly working gesture/audio layer
- **All gestures and tones act on the ROG Cetra only.** The default output device is never
  touched or switched.
- Target device is resolved from the USB ID (`0b05:1cac`) at runtime.
- **Removed the virtual `uinput` keyboard.** Volume up/down, mute and mic-mute now go
  straight to the earbuds' sink/source via `wpctl`; the volume overlay is triggered directly
  on the desktop side.
- The device is grabbed with `EVIOCGRAB`, so the dongle's hard-wired media keys no longer
  fire a second, unwanted action.

---

## First public release — before 1.0

The initial public version laid the groundwork:

- WirePlumber volume fix using software volume (`api.alsa.soft-mixer = true`), so the volume
  slider covers its full range again.
- Decoding of the vendor HID tap reports (`cc 22 43 …` with side and tap count) from the
  dongle's hidraw interface.
- Volume up/down emulated through a **virtual `uinput` keyboard** so the system/Steam overlay
  appeared, with `wpctl` as a fallback.
- Play/pause and track control via **MPRIS** (`busctl --user`).
- Fixed gesture table (1–4 taps, left/right) and tone files generated into
  `~/.cache/rog-cetra/`.

Note: this first version controlled the **system default** device for volume and played
confirmation tones on the default device as well. That is exactly what later releases
changed — everything now targets the earbuds only.

